 {:seon.task/name "a-read-key-is-one-fixnum"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime (root 21:58, 22:02). What exists [M]: a read key is a boxed Clojure vector [:ea e a] in a hash set, ~135 B a key (trial T9); a health class holds 1,522 (web T2). Measured on main [M]: 1,006 schema keys, the largest eid 1,152,920,505,196,681,680 (60 bits: Datahike's partitions use the high bits), so no split of kind + attribute + e fits a 61-bit fixnum. Rule: a key is one fixnum, a 60-bit hash of [kind a e-or-v], always, no partial packing, and STABLE across processes, versions and Jolt pins, since stored key sets are reused across them (root 22:06): e as a long through splitmix64 (pure arithmetic); a and v by our own FNV-1a-64 over canonical bytes, never Clojure's hash (not promised stable): a keyword's namespace and name as UTF-8, a v through the one codec (seon.edn/encode) to its UTF-8 bytes; a stored key set carries its format, :seon.reads/key-format 1, and a set of another format is read as no reuse; a collision only over-invalidates (the index finds the moved key's readers plus the colliding key's; it never misses); P(any collision) at 10^6 live keys ~ n^2/2^61 ~ 4e-7. A reader's key set is a sorted fxvector (8 B a key; gained/lost by a linear merge); the {key #{reader}} index is keyed by fixnum. Packed at capture and in advance! (deps may emit them from datahike.reads; else ~50 ticks a key in the core [H]). Estimate: 202 + 8k B a reader own, <= 100 B a distinct index key; 10k tracks at k = 10: ~3 MB own + ~8 MB index (boxed ~22 MB). Proofs: seon.ratom-test/a-read-key-is-equal-in-two-processes (GOLDEN values: literal fixnums in the test for a key of each kind, with a keyword, a string, a uuid and a map v, computed once when written; the core must compute exactly those, which holds across processes, restarts and Jolt pins; plus one fresh bin/jolt -e process over the same keys; a fork is no check, since it shares the hash seed (root); red under Clojure's hash); seon.ratom-test/a-track-costs-its-key-set-in-memory (k = 1, 10, 100: <= 2 x (202 + 8k) B own, <= 100 B a distinct index key, live heap after seon.lib/non-static-live!)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ratom-test/a-read-key-is-equal-in-two-processes seon.ratom-test/a-track-costs-its-key-set-in-memory}}
 {:seon.task/name "context-reads-on-the-core"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: context; runtime and flow review core changes this needs (edited by context alone; root, 2026-11-15). The owner's call (2026-11-15): bring each area's hard case and today's number to the one reactive core, fix the core where it falls short, then move the reader and delete the area's own engine in the same land. context's readers are an agent's record (seon.ctx: build, patch) and its request (seon.ctx.wire over seon.render/walk's model output). TODAY [M root]: the record is already O(d): root's record builds cold in 1.3M ticks / 4.7 MB and a real 10-datom commit patches it in 3.9k ticks / 12 KB (result/b576d928); the request is not: root's session model output 6.8B ticks / 35.8 GB cold over 506k datoms [web-2 result/b9a43662], a 30-turn agent's cold request 11B / 37.6 GB [result/a444fa99]; the move must beat these. HARD CASES for the core: (1) ORDER and an append-only prefix: the request is an ordered conversation (turns by number, a message by its link to a turn, seon.ctx/placed-messages), and a provider caches a byte-identical prefix, so a reaction's output must keep its earlier members byte-identical and add at the end; a reader that rebuilds in hash order breaks the cache. (2) PAST POINTS: a turn's page message was drawn at two past points; landing 5 (jolt/context) stores what each turn sent, so only the turn being made draws, at its head: the core needs no as-of reads from context. (3) BIG FAN-OUT: root's pull reaches 506k datoms through history joins (every eval, every turn); an [:a a] key on :seon.eval/agent or :seon.model-call/agent would wake every reader on every turn; the record's windows (seon.ctx window-moves, :seon.config.ctx/window) are how it stays bounded today. (4) PATTERN EDITS: an agent changes its own pull (swap! of :seon.ctx/pattern) and the record is rebuilt sharing every unchanged subtree (seon.ctx/share); a refine compacts root's record by unlinking members (task seon.ctx/roots-context-is-compacted-by-a-refine-that-edits-its-record): the core must take a reader whose key set shrinks. (5) UNTRACKED reads: turn-entities reads written-once facts untracked (seon.ratom/untracked) so they never become keys. (6) COLD START: an agent's first request is a cold walk; today's 6.8B is the walk's own uncontracted code, not the recording (web-2's profile); measure ticks per recorded key on it before item 3. MOVE: the record stays a fold (seon.ctx/patch is one); the request becomes a reaction over the record's members, each member's messages a cached component; reader API and order from runtime's switch-over list. RUNTIME'S ANSWER (2026-11-15): the record stays a fold (seon.ctx/patch, O(d) at 3.9k ticks); the request is reactions over the record's members, each member's messages a kept component; the ordered append-only output is a delta fold over those components in member order, a new member appending and a changed one invalidating the suffix from it; shrinking key sets are the core's store-keys! (a reaction's next run sets its new keys, drops the old); untracked written-once reads are a gap until read through the recorder. NEXT (the design gate): prototype the request reader at the REPL on root's real session against today's 6.8B before any land; context's reader is item 5 of root's switch-over list."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx.wire-cost-test/a-request-costs-what-its-commit-moved}
  :seon.task/estimate #:seon.ctx.wire{request {:n 10, :order :linear, :ticks 10000000, :bytes 50000000}}
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.ratom/a-commit-reaches-only-the-readers-of-what-it-moved"}}
 {:seon.task/name "web-reads-on-the-core"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: web (the page walk and the render proc); runtime reviews the core side. Web's section for the reactive switch-over (owner 2026-11-15: each area brings its hard cases and today's measured engine to the core; runtime folds each core requirement into seon.ratom/a-commit-reaches-only-the-readers-of-what-it-moved). WEB'S ENGINE TODAY [root's REPL]: root's page (506,583 datoms, 118 units): a cold walk 163M ticks / 0.95 GB [result/b9a43662], its layout 2.25M / 8.4 MB [ae558baa]; one real move (a new eval, 5 datoms): advance-walk! 0.93M / 4.3 MB, then laying out again 2.29M / 8.5 MB: the layout is O(page), not O(change) [ae558baa]; a move the page doesn't read: 46k. The render proc's quiet passes: 126 of 129 patched nothing, p95 1.1 s [runtime daa9d4a5]. THE NUMBER TO BEAT: ~3.2M ticks a move for one eval on root's page; lower bound the touched component's run plus its unit's html (~0.1M). HARD CASES (each tried at root's REPL; repros /tmp/web3-rt1..9.clj): (1) components read a tree (the ctx through ratom views) as well as the store; a reaction over a ref-view never reruns [d16e7b01]: views become real sources (runtime, before web's land), or components read their entity by pull (a turn's pull 9.6k, exact [c66badee]); (2) order and placement: a list keeps its members' order; a new member is an append, a moved one a move (render.proc/finish-patch); a component's place (parent, container) kept with it, a new reader placed without re-laying the page; (3) two stores in one page (main and the agent's branch), a component reading both, one index per branch store; (4) fan-out on a code change: a render redefined at a REPL reruns every component it drew (render.clj :drawn-fns), a schema change every reader (:all): a reader keyed by its render var as well as its datoms; (5) cold start: ~118 reactions on root's page (400 readers cost 8.3M ticks / 39 MB to build [c0506b14]), within today's cold walk; (6) one walk, many tabs: tabs share one walk's reactions, disposed when the view has no tab; (7) a reader that throws draws its error and the commit goes on [dc412954] (runtime: fixed in Land A); (8) the owner: the render proc commits and flushes in its step, bounded by the touched components. READER MOVE (switch-over item 2, after runtime's Land A and B): views as reactions chosen by the entity's schema on the render proc's Store; deletes page-value's view cond (render/proc.clj:857-871), the layout's O(page) re-lay, and the fixed debug/@ctx layouts as values outside the walk."
